WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.730 --> 00:00:02.450
- [Interviewer] You're the
first person I've talked to.

00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:03.410
I've heard this many times,

00:00:03.410 --> 00:00:05.420
but tell me about what
happens when you do this drug.

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:07.570
- I mean--
- Crystal meth.

00:00:07.570 --> 00:00:10.980
- Whenever you do it, man,
it's like you can fuck forever,

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:11.813
I ain't gonna lie.

00:00:11.813 --> 00:00:12.980
I mean, it's like, non-stop.

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:16.490
We can fuck for like,
from 7:00 pm to 7:00 am

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:19.250
and just be like, happy-go-lucky,
still full of energy,

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:20.770
going, running hard.

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:22.390
Not even stopping to smoke a cigarette.

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:25.320
It's like, just smoke a
cigarette while we're at it.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.150
And you've seen my old
lady, she's beautiful.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:29.120
I mean, who wouldn't wanna
go, you know what I mean?

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:30.993
So I mean, yeah.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:34.950
- All right, Lee.
- Yeah.

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:35.800
- [Interviewer] Lee,
where did you grow up?

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:36.930
Where are you from originally?

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:40.300
- I'm originally from right
here in West Virginia, Layland.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.100
I was born and raised.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:43.910
Ever since I was born, I've
been on Layland Mountain.

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:45.710
I really never moved off.

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:46.543
- [Interviewer] Tell me about your family.

00:00:46.543 --> 00:00:47.387
You had a--

00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:50.040
- My mom, she's a, my stepdad,

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:51.440
he's the preacher at the church.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.210
He passed away just not too long ago.

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:56.400
My mom, she runs the Dane
East Community Building

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:57.240
out there where we live.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:58.233
She's kinda,

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:01.360
kinda into the community with everybody.

00:01:04.443 --> 00:01:05.770
- [Interviewer] So kind
of a religious family.

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:09.320
- Yeah, I never followed
into it, but like, they was.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:11.210
My mom, she's all about
it, and my stepdad,

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:12.740
he was all about it.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:16.883
But I kinda was the, like,
never followed into it.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.563
- [Interviewer] How would
you describe your childhood?

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:21.453
- It was hit and miss.

00:01:21.453 --> 00:01:24.980
I was kinda on my own from
like, I'd say 17 and up.

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:27.520
I just kinda did my own thing.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:30.300
I kinda went out, left
mom, and went and hung out

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:32.570
with my buddies, and did everything else.

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:34.970
And then after I found
out how rough it was,

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:37.829
I kinda went back to my
grandma's house, man.

00:01:37.829 --> 00:01:40.693
I got an RV up there, I've
lived and I've moved in it.

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.650
Been doing things from there.

00:01:44.650 --> 00:01:46.760
- [Interviewer] Yeah,
Appalachia is a tough life

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:48.480
for a young man, right?
- Yes, sir.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:50.070
- Finding work?
- Yeah, you got that right.

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:52.014
There ain't nothing
around here to be doing.

00:01:52.014 --> 00:01:54.014
If you ain't logging and you ain't doing

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:58.800
some kind of power or welding
or something like that,

00:01:58.800 --> 00:01:59.870
there's nothing really for you.

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:02.680
I mean, it's kinda, it's a slow thing.

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:03.750
- [Interviewer] The coal
mines have dried up?

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:06.950
- Yeah, there ain't nobody
doing no coal mining, hardly.

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:08.150
- [Interviewer] How far
did you go in school?

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:09.750
- I went all the way up to the 11th grade

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:13.250
and my baby mama, she got
pregnant with my little boy,

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:16.270
and I dropped out, 'cause I
had to go try to figure out

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:19.063
something to kinda support
everybody else with.

00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:23.530
I went from that, I went
over to working at the

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:25.000
sawmill over here in Beckley.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:29.340
I worked there for, I'd
say, almost three years.

00:02:29.340 --> 00:02:31.930
Went from that to working
at the turkey farms

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:35.400
over in Lewisburg, I
worked there at Aviagen for

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:37.600
almost, I'd say three years there.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:38.433
And,

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:42.250
from there on, I mean,
I've kinda just went into

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:44.950
working for myself and
working for a guy doing

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:45.893
mobile detail.

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:49.440
- [Interviewer] Now, how do
you support yourself today?

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.120
- I'm doing landscaping.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:52.320
I do a lot of landscaping.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:55.240
I got a vacuum you drag
behind your lawnmower

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:58.610
and you go, it sucks up
all the leaves, acorns,

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:00.260
and everything else out of people's yards,

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:01.740
and I go over to the rich
part of the neighborhood

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:03.700
and I drag it around behind my lawnmower,

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:05.140
and I do a couple of yards,

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:07.140
and I can make pretty money doing that.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:09.180
- [Interviewer] That's
great that you're working.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:11.180
Are drugs a part of your life?

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:12.511
- They have been, yeah.

00:03:12.511 --> 00:03:16.420
I was pretty well strung
out, I was big on the ice.

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:17.980
I got into heroin for a little bit,

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:20.670
but here recently, I've
kinda got straightened out

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:22.280
a little bit, I've been
in the suboxone program

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:23.120
and it's been doing me really,

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:25.280
really good, actually.
- That's great.

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:26.113
That's great.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:30.350
And what age were you when
you started with drugs?

00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:32.893
- I was, I'd say I was probably 14.

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:35.390
- [Interviewer] And
drugs are a big deal for

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:37.390
everyone out here.
- Yeah, it's everybody.

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:40.110
I mean, anybody you run
into has either done 'em

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:41.310
or is doing 'em.
- Like the people you

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:44.530
went to school with, how
many of them got into drugs?

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:45.780
- Actually, most of the people,

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:47.290
I mean, a lot of the people
I went to school with

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:49.420
are really dead, I mean,
a lot of people died

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:50.950
from heroin, man.

00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:52.120
Heroin's dropped everybody.

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:53.376
It's crazy how many people--
- Is it the heroin

00:03:53.376 --> 00:03:54.209
or the fentanyl and the heroin?

00:03:54.209 --> 00:03:55.860
- The fentanyl, yeah.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:57.410
The fentanyl, it's dropping everybody.

00:03:57.410 --> 00:03:59.230
I was just, I think it was yesterday,

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:01.050
I found out my good buddy, John Parker,

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:03.300
he was, he just died from it.

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:05.500
They found him dead,
actually, at his house.

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:07.830
- [Interviewer] And that's
happened many times?

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:11.020
- Yeah, it's crazy how
often it happens, really.

00:04:11.020 --> 00:04:13.730
- [Interviewer] What keeps
you here in Appalachia?

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:16.790
- I just, that's a good
question, to be honest.

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:18.450
I mean, I don't really know

00:04:18.450 --> 00:04:20.790
why I stay.
- There's so few opportunities

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:23.470
and so many reasons to leave.
- I think it's just that

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:25.960
my family's here, and
I just, I'm relentless

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:26.920
to run off and leave 'em.

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:28.610
I mean, my grandma,
she needs me right now.

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:30.280
I stay up on the mountain
with her all the time,

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:32.820
and she's got diabetes and she's got the,

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:34.640
where she can't, something with her mind

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:37.110
where she can't remember,
she leaves the stove on.

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:40.010
I'm just paranoid if I actually
was to get up and leave her,

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:42.580
if she wouldn't leave the
stove on, burn the house down

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:44.677
or something like that,
you know what I mean?

00:04:44.677 --> 00:04:45.510
- [Interviewer] And you've
got a nice girlfriend.

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:47.890
- Yeah, and I got a beautiful old lady.

00:04:47.890 --> 00:04:49.740
Yeah, that's a fact.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:51.990
But if I was to leave,
she'd leave with me.

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:53.390
- [Interviewer] Yeah, you
and Sam should probably

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:55.890
think about what you could
do other than staying here,

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:57.030
especially if she gets her kid back.

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:57.933
- Yeah, no doubt.

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:03.393
- [Interviewer] Have you traveled?

00:05:03.393 --> 00:05:04.710
Have you been to other
parts of the country?

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:06.350
- I haven't been to other
parts of the country.

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:07.183
I've been out of the state.

00:05:07.183 --> 00:05:09.510
I've been to like, North
Carolina and I've been

00:05:10.559 --> 00:05:12.290
to South Carolina.

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:15.010
Myrtle Beach, Maryland.

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:17.173
Nothing major, not too far off.

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:20.250
- [Interviewer] What are
the best things about living

00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:21.083
out here?

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:23.013
Just give me some good things.

00:05:23.013 --> 00:05:24.830
It's beautiful.
- I mean, it is.

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:27.010
It's beautiful, and you got wildlife.

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:29.860
And where I live at, man, I
live so far back in the country,

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:31.290
there's really not nobody else.

00:05:31.290 --> 00:05:32.710
We don't live on top of each other like

00:05:32.710 --> 00:05:34.020
out there in the city, everybody lives

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:35.720
right dead on top of each other.

00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:37.740
Where I live at, my
closest neighbor's like,

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:39.410
half a mile up the road,
you know what I mean?

00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:42.743
- How many acres do you have?
- My family owns like, 114.

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:45.190
It's on the top of the mountain.

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:46.120
It's actually in the middle of

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:47.430
the New River National Forest.

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:49.070
The only people that
own anything around us

00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:50.880
is the Parks Service,
the park owns everything

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:52.490
that my family don't own.

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:54.480
So we're back there, we
can pretty much almost

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:57.500
gate it off to where only our
family can get back there.

00:05:57.500 --> 00:05:59.830
But really, right now, it's
open 'cause people wanna

00:05:59.830 --> 00:06:00.970
go back there and hunt Parks Service.

00:06:00.970 --> 00:06:03.150
- [Interviewer] Yeah, it's
spectacularly beautiful--

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:05.720
- Yes, that's a fact.
- Around here.

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:07.690
Some of the most beautiful
land in the country.

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:10.040
- Yeah, I mean, if you've
been up here to Grandview,

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:11.390
right above where y'all are at right now,

00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:13.180
and you go up there and
you can go to Turkey Spur

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:14.370
and you can look over.

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:16.410
If you look off of Turkey
Spur, straight across,

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:17.430
and you look over onto the mountain,

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:18.530
you're actually looking at the mountain

00:06:18.530 --> 00:06:20.080
that my family owns over there.

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:22.720
- [Interviewer] What kind of
wildlife do you have here?

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:25.800
- We got bears, we got, they
say there ain't panthers,

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:28.600
but there is panthers, big cats.

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:31.820
We got coyotes, we got
bobcats, we've got rabbits.

00:06:31.820 --> 00:06:35.130
I mean, we got rattlesnakes,
and we got copperheads.

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:36.914
It's, you name it.

00:06:36.914 --> 00:06:38.660
I've seen a little bit of
everything around here,

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:39.940
to be honest.
- And deer, elk,

00:06:39.940 --> 00:06:41.900
stuff like that?
- Yeah, we got the deer.

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:44.120
They say we got elk, I
haven't seen any elk.

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:47.670
But they say they released
elk, but I haven't seen any.

00:06:47.670 --> 00:06:51.130
But where we live at, the
Parks Service is so bad about

00:06:51.130 --> 00:06:52.310
catching all these nuisance bears

00:06:52.310 --> 00:06:53.580
over here in the city limits,

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:55.790
and they gotta take 'em
and release 'em somewhere,

00:06:55.790 --> 00:06:57.310
so they bring 'em over
there where we live at,

00:06:57.310 --> 00:06:58.450
'cause we're in the middle of nowhere,

00:06:58.450 --> 00:07:00.650
and they release 'em, and then
they become a nuisance to us,

00:07:00.650 --> 00:07:01.780
and they're up there in our trash

00:07:01.780 --> 00:07:03.630
and everything else in the nighttime.

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:06.320
You should see 'em, the other night,

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:08.810
whenever this big ole, something
was out there in the field.

00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:10.060
I couldn't tell you exactly what it was,

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:11.470
but all of the sudden, we
was laying there sleeping,

00:07:11.470 --> 00:07:12.930
and then all of the sudden,
there was this screaming,

00:07:12.930 --> 00:07:15.480
it was crazy screaming, I
was like, "what is that?"

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:17.530
It woke me up, it was screaming loud.

00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:18.367
I was like, "what is that?"

00:07:18.367 --> 00:07:20.440
And it was something
out there got attacked

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:22.110
by something in the yard,
right beside the house.

00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:24.589
It was so close it scared
the shit out of both of us.

00:07:24.589 --> 00:07:26.440
(chuckling) It was crazy.
- Yeah, you're out in

00:07:26.440 --> 00:07:27.273
the wild here.

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:31.830
What do you think got you
into this situation you're in,

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:32.730
with addiction and all that?

00:07:32.730 --> 00:07:34.130
Do you think it was the environment?

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:35.240
Was it something in your childhood?

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:36.983
Was it--
- I think it was,

00:07:36.983 --> 00:07:38.410
I think the main thing was, man,

00:07:38.410 --> 00:07:40.010
'cause there wasn't nothing else to do.

00:07:40.010 --> 00:07:41.670
- Yeah, I hear that a lot.
- 'Cause I mean, like,

00:07:41.670 --> 00:07:44.330
there really ain't nothing
to keep you, I mean, like,

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:46.053
you get high, it passes time.

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:50.440
That's really, I mean,
I can't say that there's

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:52.090
too many other reasons, other than,

00:07:52.090 --> 00:07:55.130
yeah, after I did it a couple
times, I liked the high,

00:07:55.130 --> 00:07:56.830
and it kinda just kept it rolling.

00:07:58.930 --> 00:08:01.870
Whenever I got into ice, I
didn't get addicted to ice.

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:03.430
I got addicted to the sex that

00:08:03.430 --> 00:08:05.560
me and my old lady had on ice.

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:06.770
That's what was addictive.

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:08.500
It wasn't the ice that done it for me,

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:12.280
it was the seven, eight
hours, non-stop, going at it,

00:08:12.280 --> 00:08:14.790
doing that is what got
me strung out on ice.

00:08:14.790 --> 00:08:16.310
And that was the only time, like,

00:08:16.310 --> 00:08:18.080
I'd go get it, and I knew
exactly what time it was

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:19.330
when I got home, you know what I mean?

00:08:19.330 --> 00:08:21.300
So-- (chuckling)

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:22.790
- [Interviewer] You're the
first person I've talked to.

00:08:22.790 --> 00:08:24.650
I've heard this many times,
but tell me about what happens

00:08:24.650 --> 00:08:26.540
when you do this drug.

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:28.690
- I mean--
- Crystal meth.

00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:32.110
- Whenever you do it, man,
it's like you can fuck forever.

00:08:32.110 --> 00:08:34.100
I ain't gonna lie, I
mean, it's like, non-stop.

00:08:34.100 --> 00:08:37.630
We can fuck for like,
from 7:00 pm to 7:00 am,

00:08:37.630 --> 00:08:40.380
and just be happy-go-lucky,
still full of energy,

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:41.260
going, running hard.

00:08:41.260 --> 00:08:43.510
I mean, not even stopping
to smoke a cigarette.

00:08:43.510 --> 00:08:46.420
It's like, you just smoke a
cigarette while we're at it.

00:08:46.420 --> 00:08:48.280
And you've seen my old
lady, she's beautiful.

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:50.260
I mean, who wouldn't wanna
go, you know what I mean?

00:08:50.260 --> 00:08:52.910
So I mean, yeah.

00:08:52.910 --> 00:08:57.810
I guess, she was part of
my addiction. (chuckling)

00:08:57.810 --> 00:09:00.913
- [Interviewer] And you'll
stay up for how many days?

00:09:02.040 --> 00:09:04.160
- I'm bad at, I crash at three days.

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:05.950
Three days, I'm out, it's over with.

00:09:05.950 --> 00:09:07.100
A lot of people, they can stay,

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:08.570
Sam, she can stay up a lot longer than me,

00:09:08.570 --> 00:09:10.990
but three days, I'm laying down.

00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:12.950
I can do a big ole shot
of it and I'm still,

00:09:12.950 --> 00:09:15.503
I'm laying down regardless,
I'm falling out.

00:09:15.503 --> 00:09:16.380
- [Interviewer] Yeah, but
some people stay up for--

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:17.830
- Yeah, some people, they get,

00:09:17.830 --> 00:09:20.260
I've heard of people been
up a week, and two weeks,

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:21.720
